  "articleBody": "Ever wonder why certain garages have specific drywall requirements? It's not just about aesthetics; it's a critical safety feature, especially if your garage is attached to your home or has living space above it. We're talking about fire-rated Type X drywall. This stuff is designed to slow down the spread of fire, giving you and your family precious extra minutes to get to safety if a fire starts in the garage. Think about it: a garage often houses flammable materials like gasoline, paint, and chemicals. The building codes in places like Denver and surrounding areas take this seriously, so if you're finishing a garage wall for the first time, or even just replacing drywall, you might need a permit and a specific fire rating. Getting this wrong can not only be a safety hazard but could also lead to issues down the line with inspections or even insurance. It’s always smart to have a pro check things out to make sure you’re meeting all the local requirements, especially with something as important as fire safety. If you're unsure about the right type of drywall for your garage, don't hesitate to reach out for expert advice. We can help you navigate the fire-rating requirements to ensure your home is safe and up to code.",
